sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2015-girardia-19> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The authors hypothesize that distinct planarian head shapes may be attractors in the state space of the bioelectric network formed by body cells coupled by gap junctions, that these attractors correspond to regions of morphospace in the evolutionary sense, and that octanol knocks the network's stochastic dynamics into one of several nearby attractors normally belonging to other flatworm species.";
    dc:type "conceptual";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Planarian head shapes may be attractors of a gap-junction-coupled bioelectric network";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Emmons-Bell M, Durant F, Hammelman J, Bessonov N, Volpert V, Morokuma J, Pinet K, Adams DS, Pietak A, Lobo D, Levin M, “Gap Junctional Blockade Stochastically Induces Different Species-Specific Head Anatomies in Genetically Wild-Type Girardia dorotocephala Flatworms”, International journal of molecular sciences (2015), doi:10.3390/ijms161126065; section “3. Discussion › 3.7. A hypothesis: Physiological Network Attractors Underlie Regions of Anatomical Morphospace”. Scope: Planarian body-wide bioelectric networks (G. dorotocephala and related species)"
}
